{
  "note": "The tier screens, re-run 27 Aug 2026 on the corrected --spreading boundary at the re-centred density (0.60 W/mm^2) and ACROSS THE dt_max LADDER. These twelve shapes had no batch script, were never in the P0.5 catalogue plan, and existed only at the superseded boundary -- see findings_harvest_correction.json. They now have a script: scripts/recentred_gapfill.sh. Two of generation 0's five phase-1 floorplan metrics come from here (docs/LADDER_GEN0.md section 3), and this re-run changes what one of them can be.",
  "configuration": {
    "cores": 34,
    "density_W_per_mm2": 0.6,
    "cfm": 88,
    "burial_um": 200,
    "stack": "spec:package=direct_die,mr=none,src=200,cell=50",
    "spreading": true,
    "n_blocks": 1126
  },
  "why_dt_max_was_swept": "examples/thermal_tiers.py hardcoded --dt-max 10.0 -- the unsourced LEGACY envelope -- until 27 Aug 2026, against a DEMONSTRATED 45 K. dt_max defines the plateau metric, so every plateau this driver has ever reported was measured at a hidden ceiling. Sweeping it turns an assumption into a measurement.",
  "FINDING_1_the_plateau_metric_degenerates_at_the_demonstrated_envelope": {
    "what": "At dt_max = 45 K EVERY workload shape returns plateau = the whole die (1126 of 1126 blocks, one shape 1125). At dt_max = 10 K the same shapes span 4 to 56 blocks -- a 14x spread, and a genuinely discriminating screen.",
    "why": "The die's OWN temperature spread is 31.9-45.0 K, i.e. smaller than or equal to the demonstrated 45 K lift. 'Blocks within dt_max of the peak' therefore evaluates to 'all blocks', always, and the metric carries no information.",
    "consequence_for_gen_0": "docs/LADDER_GEN0.md section 3 lists 'plateau width at dt_max' as one of five phase-1 floorplan metrics, 'produced by the tier screens'. At the demonstrated envelope it is UNUSABLE as a floorplan discriminator and must be redefined or dropped. A workable redefinition: plateau within a fraction of the die's own temperature range, or within the per-tile achievable dT rather than the device maximum.",
    "and_it_agrees_with_something_measured_independently": "The die-average strategy study found that at large budgets proportional extraction beats hotspot clipping, because a targeted plan can only pull the peak to the runner-up's level. This is the same statement from the other side: once lift is large relative to the die's spread, 'which blocks are within reach' stops being the question and 'where to spend the watts' becomes it."
  },
  "FINDING_2_clip_one_gain_is_independent_of_dt_max": {
    "what": "Clip-one gain is IDENTICAL to six decimal places across the entire dt_max ladder for every shape.",
    "why": "gain = T[0] - max(T[0] - dt_max, T[1]). Whenever the runner-up lies within dt_max of the peak -- which it always does here, since the die's whole spread is <= 45 K -- this reduces to T[0] - T[1], the peak-to-runner-up gap, in which dt_max does not appear.",
    "consequence_for_gen_0": "The two metrics LADDER_GEN0 attributes to the tier screens are not two independent metrics at this envelope: 'clip-one gain' IS 'peak-to-runner-up gap'. The good news is that this one is robust, device-independent and discriminating -- 1.18 K (fpu2) to 8.07 K (g4_turbo_idle), a 6.8x spread across shapes.",
    "peak_to_runner_up_gap_by_shape_K": {
      "fpu2": 1.179,
      "fpu3": 2.9467,
      "fpu4": 3.9836,
      "g4_mixed25": 7.5717,
